## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit data manipulation, endianness, and composing simple bit tricks to achieve a larger transformation without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value composed of two 64-bit halves
- Byte reversal within 64-bit words (endian swap)
- Combining results to form final 128-bit reversal
- No loops; relies on fixed-mask shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}

void endianSwap128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = reverse64(lo);
  *out_lo = reverse64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify correctness for non-trivial patterns quickly?
- How does this approach scale if you extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of efficient bit-hacks and how to compute parity without loops or branching, which is common in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- Bit folding using XOR and shifts to aggregate bit information
- Constant-time parity methods and small lookup tables
- Branchless implementation for predictable performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t parity64(uint64_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return (0x6996 >> (x & 0xF)) & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit values?
- Compare with using builtin parity intrinsics in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive a compact, branch-free bit-twisting routine for combinatorial generation.

## Key Concepts

- Next permutation with fixed popcount via snoob
- Branchless 64-bit arithmetic and overflow behavior
- Edge-case handling in tests

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextWithSameBitCount(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
  ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for arbitrary precision (128-bit) using two limbs?
- How would you detect when no next permutation exists efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Interview context explanation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit as two 64-bit halves; parallel popcount masks; integer promotion.
- No loops; constant-time composition.

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  auto pc64 = [](uint64_t v){
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0x7F;
  };
  return pc64(lo) + pc64(hi);
}
```

