## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests branchless bit-twiddling and cross-half reasoning on a 128-bit type. Parity is a classic low-level primitive with real-world use in checksums and error-detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless parity computation
- XOR folding techniques across 64-bit halves
- 128-bit struct handling without loops

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo ^= lo >> 32;
  lo ^= lo >> 16;
  lo ^= lo >> 8;
  lo ^= lo >> 4;
  lo ^= lo >> 2;
  lo ^= lo >> 1;
  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi ^= hi >> 32;
  hi ^= hi >> 16;
  hi ^= hi >> 8;
  hi ^= hi >> 4;
  hi ^= hi >> 2;
  hi ^= hi >> 1;
  return (lo & 1) ^ (hi & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to count parity modulo any odd number (e.g., 3) with similar tricks?
- Could you prove the branchless reduction yields correct parity for all 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate multi-word integers with precise bit logic, no loops, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit two-word shifts without loops
- x & ~(x<<1) to locate run-start bits
- 64-bit popcount for final tally

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t countOneRuns128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t lo_shift = lo << 1;
  uint64_t hi_shift = (hi << 1) | (lo >> 63);
  uint64_t mask_lo = lo & ~lo_shift;
  uint64_t mask_hi = hi & ~hi_shift;
  return __builtin_popcountll(mask_lo) + __builtin_popcountll(mask_hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for counting 0-runs?
- What's the time complexity compared to a loop-based solution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit values without hardware helpers.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation as hi/lo
- Leading-zero counting
- Branching vs branchless approaches
- Correct handling of zero input

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo helper assuming a 64-bit lz routine exists as lz64
static int lz64(uint64_t v){
  int n = 0;
  if ((v & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) == 0) { n += 32; v <<= 32; }
  if ((v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 16; v <<= 16; }
  if ((v & 0xFF00000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 8; v <<= 8; }
  if ((v & 0xF000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 4; v <<= 4; }
  if ((v & 0xC000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 2; v <<= 2; }
  if ((v & 0x8000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 1; }
  return n;
}
int countLeadingZeros128(uint128 x){
  if (x.hi != 0) return lz64(x.hi);
  if (x.lo != 0) return 64 + lz64(x.lo);
  return 128;
}
```

